db/ref 17592186180398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179831 #edb/ref 1044 #edb/ref 17592186045463 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179831 #edb/ref 1045 "Solving the Logistic Growth Equation" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179831 #edb/ref 1046 #edb/ref 17592186180353 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179831 #edb/ref 1049 #edb/ref 17592186050442 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186179831 #edb/ref 1053 #uuid "0e333d7b-9586-5dcc-a2e9-29e8919f1fde" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180353 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180397 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180353 #edb/ref 1061 1924 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180353 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "3acea89b-a4db-5284-92df-1f9665ae536c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180353 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180792 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180396 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180398 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180396 #edb/ref 1061 27476 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180396 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "a534edd7-0a0d-55a8-a499-ecfc5a548c59" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180396 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179676 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180397 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186180396 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180397 #edb/ref 1061 27483 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180397 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "b4f05448-8bec-515f-b01f-973c815d0ffd" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180397 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186179677 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180398 #edb/ref 1061 27816 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180398 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8237a905-9644-5c44-a21f-8bcd1b0638a5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180398 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186180682 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180682 #edb/ref 1064 "The general solution to the logistic growth equation\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm{d} y}{\\mathrm{d} t} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nis given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\dfrac{a}{1+Ce^{-akt}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C>0$ is an integration constant. Let's now derive this formula.\n\nOur differential equation is separable, so we start by separating variables and then integrating with respect to $t\\mathbin{:}$ \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{dy}{dt} & =ky(a-y) \\\\ \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\frac{dy}{dt} & =k \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\frac{dy}{dt}\\,\\text{d}t & =\\int k\\,\\text{d}t \\\\ \\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, using the fact that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{ 1}{y(a-y)} = \\dfrac{1}{a}\\left(\\dfrac{1}{y} + \\dfrac{1}{a-y}\\right),\n$$\n\nwe can rewrite the integrand on the left-hand side and complete the integration, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{1}{y(a-y)}\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ \\frac{1}{a}\\int (\\frac{1}{y}+\\frac{1}{a-y})\\,dy & =\\int k\\,dt \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y}-\\frac{1}{y-a})\\,dy & =ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ -\\int (\\frac{1}{y-a}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,dy & =ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\int (\\frac{1}{y-a}-\\frac{1}{y})\\,dy & =-ak\\int \\,dt \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|y-a|-\\operatorname{ln}|y| & =-akt+{C}_{1} \\\\ \\operatorname{ln}|\\frac{y-a}{y}| & =-akt+{C}_{1} \\\\ |\\frac{y-a}{y}| & ={e}^{-akt+{C}_{1}} \\\\ |\\frac{y-a}{y}| & ={e}^{{C}_{1}}{e}^{-akt} \\\\ \\frac{y-a}{y} & =\\pm {e}^{{C}_{1}}{e}^{-akt} \\\\ \\frac{y-a}{y} & =\\pm C{e}^{-akt}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere $C=e^{C_1}$ is a positive constant. \n\nIn our modeling, we have assumed that $0 < y  < a.$ Therefore, the left-hand side of the equation must be negative since the numerator is negative and the denominator is positive. Therefore, we discard the positive option, and we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{y-a}{y}  =  -C e^{-akt}.\n$$\n\nSolving this equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{y-a}{y} & =-C{e}^{-akt} \\\\ y-a & =-Cy{e}^{-akt} \\\\ y+y\\cdot C{e}^{-akt} & =a \\\\ y(1+C{e}^{-akt}) & =a \\\\ y & =\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}\\,\\,\\,.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180682 #edb/ref 1065 4521 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180682 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "ba0b74de-5c49-5d2e-a9a6-207220dbb90e" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180682 #edb/ref 1067 "Deriving the General Solution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180792 #edb/ref 1064 "Recall that a differential equation of the form\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\mathrm dy}{\\mathrm dt} = ky(a-y)\n$$\n\nis called a logistic growth equation. The positive constant $a > 0$ is the carrying capacity, and $k > 0$ is a constant of proportionality.\n\nA typical solution curve $y = y(t)$ to this equation is shown below. \n\n![](images/0f/0f416479bbf72d3b9c1bf48861883e0e77bbc1c1d60d7fa48fb89e6f21c104eb.png)\n\nIt can be shown that the general solution to this equation is given by\n\n$$\ny(t) = \\frac{a}{1+C e^{-ak t}}\n$$\n\nwhere $C$ is an integration constant. \n\nNote the following properties of this general solution:\n\n- Since $a$ and $k$ are both positive, $e^{-akt}\\to0$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ Therefore, $y(t)\\to a$ as $t\\to\\infty.$ In other words, $y(t)$ approaches the carrying capacity as $t$ increases. This agrees with the behavior shown in our diagram.\n\n- Since $e^{-akt}\\to\\infty$ as $t\\to-\\infty,$ we have that $y(t)\\to 0$ as $t\\to-\\infty.$ This also agrees with our diagram.\n\nWe can use the general solution of the logistic growth equation to make predictions about a population governed by logistic growth. Let's see how." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180792 #edb/ref 1065 981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180792 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "7ba42b62-b5a3-5c54-8a92-9ce67945fd36" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186180792 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351658 #edb/ref 1080 "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-0.1t}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351658 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351658 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "0a7d2a3a-2e9b-513d-aefc-deb7330c3256" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351965 #edb/ref 1080 "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+4{e}^{-2t}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351965 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186351965 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "2bd4e14d-f873-5392-91f7-897cf31de18f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353293 #edb/ref 1080 "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-0.5t}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353293 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353293 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "c73b56a5-cac1-5b5a-8072-9761fae2a107" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353464 #edb/ref 1080 "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.5t}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353464 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353464 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "da7e22be-49d9-5065-8d9b-091acb94dba2" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353579 #edb/ref 1080 "P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+5{e}^{-0.2t}}" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353579 #edb/ref 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353579 #edb/ref 1083 #uuid "e700e7cb-c861-580f-9a2b-f525130e76a7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186351658 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186351965 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353293 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353981 #edb/ref 1075 #edb/ref 17592186353579 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353981 #edb/ref 1076 #edb/ref 17592186045493 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353981 #edb/ref 1077 "selection" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353981 #edb/ref 1078 #edb/ref 17592186353464 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186353981 #edb/ref 1079 #uuid "5ce8c811-8797-5860-954a-946f44e6804b" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354261 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186353981 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354261 #edb/ref 1069 "q-83875" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354261 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354261 #edb/ref 1071 "The growth rate of the mouse population $P(t)$ in a certain field is jointly proportional to the mouse population at time $t,$ measured in years, and the difference between the field's carrying capacity and the mouse population. If the constant of proportionality is $0.000\\,062\\,5$ and the carrying capacity is $8\\,000,$ then find an expression for the mouse population after $t$ years, given that there were $1\\,000$ mice in the field initially." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354261 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "2c1c2a21-498a-5ae4-81c4-0f8dccba7406" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186354261 #edb/ref 1074 "Since the mouse population's growth rate is *jointly* proportional to the mouse population at time $t$ and the difference between the carrying capacity and the mouse population, the mouse population follows a logistic growth model.\n\nA logistic growth equation has the form\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=kP(a-P),\n$$\n\nwhere $k>0$ is a constant of proportionality, and $a>P$ is the carrying capacity.\n\nThe general solution to the logistic growth equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{a}{1+C{e}^{-akt}}.\n$$\n\nIn this case, the constant of proportionality is $k=0.000\\,062\\,5,$ and the carrying capacity is $a=8\\,000.$ Therefore, the mouse population growth is given by the equation\n\n$$\n\\frac{dP}{dt}=0.000\\,062\\,5P(8\\,000-P).\n$$\n\nThe general solution to this equation is\n\n$$\nP(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-(8\\,000\\cdot 0.000\\,062\\,5)t}}=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5t}}.\n$$\n\nWe now substitute the initial condition $P(0)=1\\,000$ and solve for $C.$ We get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{8\\,000}{1+C{e}^{-0.5\\cdot 0}} & =1\\,000 \\\\ 1+C & =\\frac{8\\,000}{1\\,000} \\\\ C & =8-1 \\\\  & =7.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(t)=\\frac{8\\,000}{1+7{e}^{-0.5t}}.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186429139 #edb/ref 1030 0.3333333333333333 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429139 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account differential: 1 rises / 3 eligible passed source activities = 0.333333; nearest selected fraction = 1/3. Crossing activity numbers [3]; waits [3]. Separate account estimates: differential=1/3 (1/3). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: True. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/differential-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3674->1163. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186429139 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048578 #edb/ref 17592186045436]}
